{"module":"colonizers","voice":"female","duration":323.4,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"The colonizers: Portugal, Spain, Britain and the empire system","start":0.0,"end":4.54,"words":[[0.04,0.18],[0.18,0.95],[1.2,1.83],[2.07,2.49],[2.74,3.16],[3.16,3.37],[3.37,3.51],[3.51,3.93],[3.93,4.35]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Columbus was one person in a system that ran for four centuries.","start":5.34,"end":9.45,"words":[[5.34,5.92],[5.92,6.14],[6.14,6.36],[6.36,6.73],[6.73,6.88],[6.88,7.02],[7.02,7.46],[7.46,7.68],[7.68,7.9],[7.9,8.05],[8.05,8.19],[8.19,8.92]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Each empire added a piece of the machinery: the trade, the legal theory, the plantation code, the racial categories.","start":9.8,"end":18.26,"words":[[9.85,10.08],[10.08,10.55],[10.55,10.86],[10.86,11.02],[11.02,11.25],[11.25,11.41],[11.41,11.56],[11.56,12.26],[12.53,12.69],[12.69,13.16],[13.43,13.59],[13.59,13.97],[13.97,14.44],[14.71,14.87],[14.87,15.65],[15.65,16.04],[16.31,16.47],[16.47,16.93],[16.93,17.71]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Together they built the world the CAREN Act is still responding to.","start":18.61,"end":22.61,"words":[[18.65,19.1],[19.1,19.32],[19.32,19.62],[19.62,19.76],[19.76,20.06],[20.06,20.21],[20.21,20.58],[20.58,20.8],[20.8,20.95],[20.95,21.25],[21.25,21.91],[21.91,22.13]]},{"el":"h0","text":"Portugal opens the Atlantic trade","start":22.96,"end":25.36,"words":[[23.01,23.64],[23.64,24.11],[24.11,24.27],[24.27,24.9],[24.9,25.29]]},{"el":"s0","text":"In 1444, at Lagos in Portugal, about 235 captive Africans were divided and sold in a public square, an event the royal chronicler Gomes Eanes de Zurara described in detail.","start":26.06,"end":40.03,"words":[[26.12,26.27],[26.27,27.2],[27.47,27.62],[27.62,28.09],[28.09,28.24],[28.24,28.94],[29.21,29.59],[29.59,31.06],[31.06,31.52],[31.52,32.14],[32.14,32.29],[32.29,32.91],[32.91,33.14],[33.14,33.53],[33.53,33.68],[33.68,33.83],[33.83,34.3],[34.3,34.76],[35.03,35.18],[35.18,35.57],[35.57,35.72],[35.72,36.11],[36.11,36.73],[36.73,37.11],[37.11,37.34],[37.34,37.5],[37.5,38.11],[38.11,38.81],[38.81,38.96],[38.96,39.5]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Portugal built Elmina castle in 1482 and traded along the African coast.","start":40.38,"end":46.39,"words":[[40.4,41.07],[41.07,41.4],[41.4,41.91],[41.91,42.32],[42.32,42.49],[42.49,43.41],[43.41,43.67],[43.67,44.25],[44.25,44.59],[44.59,44.76],[44.76,45.34],[45.34,45.84]]},{"el":"s0","text":"In 1526, King Afonso I of Kongo, himself a Christian ally of Portugal, wrote to Portugal's king protesting that traders were kidnapping his people, “even noblemen and the sons of noblemen.”","start":46.74,"end":59.42,"words":[[46.79,46.93],[46.93,48.1],[48.34,48.55],[48.55,49.03],[49.03,49.17],[49.17,49.31],[49.31,49.79],[50.03,50.52],[50.52,50.66],[50.66,51.21],[51.21,51.48],[51.48,51.62],[51.62,52.24],[52.48,52.76],[52.76,52.9],[52.9,53.52],[53.52,53.73],[53.73,54.35],[54.35,54.55],[54.55,55.04],[55.04,55.18],[55.18,55.73],[55.73,55.94],[55.94,56.35],[56.59,56.87],[56.87,57.49],[57.49,57.7],[57.7,57.83],[57.83,58.11],[58.11,58.25],[58.25,58.94]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Portuguese and Brazilian ships carried about 5.8 million Africans across the Atlantic, more than any other nation.","start":59.77,"end":67.56,"words":[[59.82,60.41],[60.41,60.64],[60.64,61.3],[61.3,61.6],[61.6,61.97],[61.97,62.34],[62.34,63.24],[63.24,63.68],[63.68,64.28],[64.28,64.65],[64.65,64.8],[64.8,65.46],[65.72,65.87],[65.87,66.09],[66.09,66.32],[66.32,66.54],[66.54,67.06]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Brazil was the largest destination and the last country in the Americas to abolish slavery, in 1888.","start":67.91,"end":75.5,"words":[[67.96,68.41],[68.41,68.64],[68.64,68.79],[68.79,69.33],[69.33,70.16],[70.16,70.39],[70.39,70.54],[70.54,70.84],[70.84,71.3],[71.3,71.45],[71.45,71.6],[71.6,72.21],[72.21,72.36],[72.36,72.82],[72.82,73.5],[73.77,73.92],[73.92,74.98]]},{"el":"h1","text":"Spain asks whether Native people are fully human","start":76.4,"end":79.55,"words":[[76.45,76.87],[76.87,77.21],[77.21,77.55],[77.55,78.06],[78.06,78.48],[78.48,78.56],[78.56,78.9],[78.9,79.41]]},{"el":"s1","text":"From 1513, Spanish conquistadors read the Requerimiento aloud, in Spanish, to people who couldn't understand it, demanding submission to the Crown and the Church, and declaring that any resulting deaths were their own fault.","start":80.25,"end":96.08,"words":[[80.26,80.58],[80.58,81.54],[81.83,82.31],[82.31,83.19],[83.19,83.43],[83.43,83.59],[83.59,84.72],[84.72,85.2],[85.48,85.64],[85.64,86.21],[86.49,86.65],[86.65,87.05],[87.05,87.21],[87.21,87.69],[87.69,88.42],[88.42,88.66],[88.94,89.58],[89.58,90.23],[90.23,90.39],[90.39,90.55],[90.55,90.95],[90.95,91.19],[91.19,91.35],[91.35,91.83],[92.11,92.36],[92.36,93.08],[93.08,93.32],[93.32,93.56],[93.56,94.2],[94.2,94.53],[94.53,94.69],[94.69,94.93],[94.93,95.17],[95.17,95.57]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Native people were assigned to Spaniards as forced laborers under the encomienda.","start":96.43,"end":101.1,"words":[[96.48,96.88],[96.88,97.2],[97.2,97.33],[97.33,97.72],[97.72,97.85],[97.85,98.44],[98.44,98.57],[98.57,98.83],[98.83,99.35],[99.35,99.61],[99.61,99.74],[99.74,100.59]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Bartolomé de las Casas, a former encomendero turned friar, spent fifty years denouncing it; the New Laws of 1542 limited it.","start":101.45,"end":111.34,"words":[[101.52,102.23],[102.23,102.39],[102.39,102.63],[102.63,103.1],[103.37,103.53],[103.53,103.85],[103.85,104.87],[104.87,105.19],[105.19,105.66],[105.94,106.33],[106.33,106.73],[106.73,107.04],[107.04,107.75],[107.75,107.99],[108.26,108.42],[108.42,108.66],[108.66,108.89],[108.89,109.05],[109.05,110.0],[110.0,110.55],[110.55,110.79]]},{"el":"s1","text":"At the Valladolid debate (1550–51), Las Casas argued against Juan Ginés de Sepúlveda, who claimed, citing Aristotle, that Native people were “natural slaves.”","start":111.69,"end":123.94,"words":[[111.74,111.9],[111.9,112.05],[112.05,112.73],[112.73,113.19],[113.19,114.86],[115.13,115.35],[115.35,115.73],[115.73,116.12],[116.12,116.57],[116.57,116.88],[116.88,117.41],[117.41,117.56],[117.56,118.32],[118.59,118.74],[118.74,119.27],[119.54,119.99],[119.99,120.75],[121.02,121.25],[121.25,121.7],[121.7,122.08],[122.08,122.24],[122.24,122.84],[122.84,123.38]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Spain's colonies later sorted people into dozens of mixed-ancestry castas, recorded in official paintings.","start":124.29,"end":131.26,"words":[[124.34,124.78],[124.78,125.29],[125.29,125.65],[125.65,126.02],[126.02,126.38],[126.38,126.67],[126.67,127.11],[127.11,127.25],[127.25,128.2],[128.2,128.71],[128.97,129.47],[129.47,129.62],[129.62,130.06],[130.06,130.71]]},{"el":"h2","text":"Where did “race” begin?","start":132.16,"end":133.53,"words":[[132.21,132.38],[132.38,132.56],[132.56,132.79],[132.79,133.14]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Some historians trace modern race to Spain's limpieza de sangre (“purity of blood”) statutes, which from the 1400s barred people with Jewish or Muslim ancestors from offices even after conversion, making ancestry, not belief, the test.","start":134.23,"end":149.96,"words":[[134.23,134.45],[134.45,135.2],[135.2,135.58],[135.58,135.95],[135.95,136.25],[136.25,136.4],[136.4,136.85],[136.85,137.53],[137.53,137.68],[137.68,138.05],[138.05,138.65],[138.65,138.81],[138.81,139.11],[139.37,140.04],[140.31,140.61],[140.61,140.91],[140.91,141.06],[141.06,142.03],[142.03,142.26],[142.26,142.63],[142.63,142.86],[142.86,143.23],[143.23,143.31],[143.31,143.76],[143.76,144.36],[144.36,144.66],[144.66,145.11],[145.11,145.41],[145.41,145.71],[145.71,146.38],[146.65,147.1],[147.1,147.77],[148.03,148.26],[148.26,148.71],[148.97,149.12],[149.12,149.5]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Others see race emerging mainly from Atlantic slavery and English colonial law.","start":150.31,"end":155.42,"words":[[150.36,150.64],[150.64,150.78],[150.78,151.05],[151.05,151.54],[151.54,151.96],[151.96,152.24],[152.24,152.79],[152.79,153.35],[153.35,153.56],[153.56,153.98],[153.98,154.67],[154.67,154.88]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Most agree it formed between about 1450 and 1700, which is the argument of the module “Before race.”","start":155.77,"end":163.43,"words":[[155.82,156.2],[156.2,156.5],[156.5,156.65],[156.65,156.96],[156.96,157.42],[157.42,157.8],[157.8,158.56],[158.56,158.78],[158.78,160.0],[160.27,160.57],[160.57,160.72],[160.72,160.88],[160.88,161.49],[161.49,161.64],[161.64,161.79],[161.79,162.25],[162.25,162.55],[162.55,162.93]]},{"el":"h3","text":"Britain writes the plantation code","start":164.33,"end":166.46,"words":[[164.37,164.81],[164.81,165.18],[165.18,165.33],[165.33,166.07],[166.07,166.37]]},{"el":"s3","text":"John Hawkins led England's first slaving voyages in the 1560s, later with Queen Elizabeth I's backing.","start":167.16,"end":175.67,"words":[[167.21,167.56],[167.56,168.08],[168.08,168.34],[168.34,169.05],[169.05,169.4],[169.4,170.01],[170.01,170.71],[170.71,170.89],[170.89,171.06],[171.06,172.29],[172.6,173.03],[173.03,173.3],[173.3,173.65],[173.65,174.35],[174.35,174.61],[174.61,175.14]]},{"el":"s3","text":"The Royal African Company, chartered under the future James II, shipped more enslaved Africans than any other single company; many were branded with “DY” for the Duke of York.","start":176.02,"end":187.16,"words":[[176.07,176.21],[176.21,176.58],[176.58,177.1],[177.1,177.69],[177.94,178.39],[178.39,178.68],[178.68,178.83],[178.83,179.27],[179.27,179.71],[179.71,180.38],[180.63,180.93],[180.93,181.08],[181.08,181.67],[181.67,182.26],[182.26,182.48],[182.48,182.7],[182.7,182.92],[182.92,183.36],[183.36,183.95],[184.21,184.5],[184.5,184.65],[184.65,185.17],[185.17,185.39],[185.39,185.61],[185.61,185.76],[185.76,185.9],[185.9,186.2],[186.2,186.35],[186.35,186.64]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Barbados's 1661 slave code became the model for Jamaica's and South Carolina's.","start":187.51,"end":193.42,"words":[[187.56,188.25],[188.25,189.35],[189.35,189.69],[189.69,189.97],[189.97,190.38],[190.38,190.52],[190.52,190.86],[190.86,191.0],[191.0,191.62],[191.62,191.82],[191.82,192.1],[192.1,192.85]]},{"el":"s3","text":"British ships carried about 3.2 million Africans in all.","start":193.77,"end":197.57,"words":[[193.81,194.21],[194.21,194.48],[194.48,194.82],[194.82,195.16],[195.16,195.83],[195.83,196.24],[196.24,196.78],[196.78,196.91],[196.91,197.11]]},{"el":"s3","text":"In 1781 the crew of the slave ship Zong threw about 130 captives overboard, and the owners sued their insurers for the loss as “cargo.”","start":197.92,"end":207.4,"words":[[197.96,198.11],[198.11,199.24],[199.24,199.39],[199.39,199.61],[199.61,199.77],[199.77,199.92],[199.92,200.29],[200.29,200.52],[200.52,200.75],[200.75,200.97],[200.97,201.35],[201.35,202.55],[202.55,203.08],[203.08,203.69],[203.95,204.18],[204.18,204.33],[204.33,204.7],[204.7,204.93],[204.93,205.16],[205.16,205.76],[205.76,205.91],[205.91,206.06],[206.06,206.29],[206.29,206.44],[206.44,206.89]]},{"el":"s3","text":"The first Africans in English Virginia arrived in 1619 aboard the White Lion, an English privateer that had seized them from a Portuguese ship carrying captives from Angola.","start":207.75,"end":219.49,"words":[[207.8,207.96],[207.96,208.27],[208.27,208.88],[208.88,209.04],[209.04,209.5],[209.5,210.12],[210.12,210.59],[210.59,210.74],[210.74,211.82],[211.82,212.13],[212.13,212.29],[212.29,212.6],[212.6,213.06],[213.33,213.49],[213.49,213.95],[213.95,214.65],[214.65,214.88],[214.88,215.11],[215.11,215.42],[215.42,215.65],[215.65,215.96],[215.96,216.12],[216.12,216.74],[216.74,216.97],[216.97,217.51],[217.51,218.05],[218.05,218.36],[218.36,218.98]]},{"el":"h4","text":"France and the Netherlands","start":220.39,"end":222.33,"words":[[220.43,220.89],[220.89,221.17],[221.17,221.36],[221.36,222.2]]},{"el":"s4","text":"The Dutch West India Company traded enslaved Africans to the Americas and used enslaved labor in New Amsterdam (New York).","start":223.03,"end":230.59,"words":[[223.08,223.22],[223.22,223.51],[223.51,223.8],[223.8,224.23],[224.23,224.73],[224.73,225.24],[225.24,225.81],[225.81,226.39],[226.39,226.53],[226.53,226.68],[226.68,227.25],[227.25,227.47],[227.47,227.76],[227.76,228.33],[228.33,228.69],[228.69,228.84],[228.84,229.05],[229.05,229.63],[229.63,229.84],[229.84,230.13]]},{"el":"s4","text":"France's Code Noir (1685) governed its colonies; Saint-Domingue, today's Haiti, became the most profitable colony in the world on the labor of enslaved Africans, until they overthrew the French (see “Before race” and “A world system”).","start":230.94,"end":246.47,"words":[[230.96,231.48],[231.48,231.78],[231.78,232.0],[232.0,233.12],[233.38,233.83],[233.83,234.05],[234.05,234.65],[234.91,235.8],[236.06,236.51],[236.51,236.96],[237.22,237.67],[237.67,237.82],[237.82,238.19],[238.19,238.93],[238.93,239.38],[239.38,239.53],[239.53,239.68],[239.68,239.98],[239.98,240.13],[240.13,240.28],[240.28,240.65],[240.65,240.8],[240.8,241.39],[241.39,242.06],[242.33,242.7],[242.7,242.92],[242.92,243.44],[243.44,243.59],[243.59,244.04],[244.04,244.19],[244.19,244.49],[244.49,244.79],[244.79,245.01],[245.01,245.16],[245.16,245.46],[245.46,245.98]]},{"el":"h5","text":"The United States becomes an empire","start":247.37,"end":249.9,"words":[[247.41,247.57],[247.57,248.19],[248.19,248.65],[248.65,249.12],[249.12,249.27],[249.27,249.74]]},{"el":"s5","text":"In 1893, American businessmen backed by U.S. Marines overthrew Hawaii's Queen Liliʻuokalani; Congress formally apologized in 1993.","start":250.6,"end":262.04,"words":[[250.65,250.81],[250.81,251.99],[252.26,252.89],[252.89,253.6],[253.6,253.91],[253.91,254.15],[254.15,254.54],[254.93,255.41],[255.41,255.96],[255.96,256.51],[256.51,256.82],[256.82,257.92],[258.19,258.74],[258.74,259.22],[259.22,260.08],[260.08,260.24],[260.24,261.57]]},{"el":"s5","text":"After 1898 the U.S. took Puerto Rico, Guam and the Philippines.","start":262.39,"end":267.83,"words":[[262.44,262.72],[262.72,263.8],[263.8,263.94],[263.94,264.3],[264.66,264.87],[264.87,265.37],[265.37,265.8],[266.05,266.34],[266.34,266.55],[266.55,266.69],[266.69,267.34]]},{"el":"s5","text":"Rudyard Kipling's poem “The White Man's Burden” (1899) urged Americans to rule the Philippines, where a war against independence fighters killed hundreds of thousands of Filipinos by most estimates.","start":268.18,"end":280.92,"words":[[268.23,268.68],[268.68,269.19],[269.19,269.56],[269.56,269.71],[269.71,270.01],[270.01,270.3],[270.3,270.67],[270.67,271.78],[272.04,272.33],[272.33,273.0],[273.0,273.15],[273.15,273.37],[273.37,273.52],[273.52,274.18],[274.44,274.66],[274.66,274.81],[274.81,274.96],[274.96,275.4],[275.4,276.21],[276.21,276.66],[276.66,276.95],[276.95,277.54],[277.54,277.69],[277.69,278.28],[278.28,278.43],[278.43,279.17],[279.17,279.39],[279.39,279.76],[279.76,280.42]]},{"el":"s5","text":"In the Insular Cases (1901) the Supreme Court called Puerto Rico “foreign … in a domestic sense”; its residents are U.S. citizens who still can't vote for president.","start":281.27,"end":291.71,"words":[[281.32,281.46],[281.46,281.6],[281.6,282.08],[282.08,282.5],[282.5,283.33],[283.58,283.71],[283.71,284.13],[284.13,284.34],[284.34,284.62],[284.62,285.1],[285.1,285.45],[285.45,285.8],[285.8,285.87],[285.87,286.01],[286.01,286.14],[286.14,286.7],[286.7,287.05],[287.29,287.5],[287.5,288.12],[288.12,288.19],[288.19,288.54],[288.89,289.44],[289.44,289.58],[289.58,289.86],[289.86,290.14],[290.14,290.42],[290.42,290.55],[290.55,291.25]]},{"el":"h6","text":"The common pattern","start":292.61,"end":293.72,"words":[[292.66,292.81],[292.81,293.2],[293.2,293.59]]},{"el":"s6","text":"Across five centuries and every empire, the same tools appear: claim the land (discovery), classify people by ancestry or color, take their labor, write it into law, justify it with religion and then science, and divide the colonized from one another.","start":294.42,"end":310.42,"words":[[294.47,294.83],[294.83,295.11],[295.11,295.76],[295.76,295.97],[295.97,296.26],[296.26,296.76],[297.01,297.15],[297.15,297.44],[297.44,297.72],[297.72,298.08],[298.33,298.69],[298.69,298.83],[298.83,299.11],[299.11,299.83],[300.08,300.65],[300.65,301.01],[301.01,301.22],[301.22,301.79],[301.79,301.86],[301.86,302.22],[302.47,302.76],[302.76,302.97],[302.97,303.4],[303.65,303.94],[303.94,304.08],[304.08,304.37],[304.37,304.58],[304.83,305.47],[305.47,305.62],[305.62,305.83],[305.83,306.4],[306.4,306.62],[306.62,306.83],[306.83,307.33],[307.58,307.79],[307.79,308.22],[308.22,308.37],[308.37,309.01],[309.01,309.29],[309.29,309.51],[309.51,309.94]]},{"el":"s6","text":"Resistance appears everywhere too, from Kongo's king to Haiti's revolutionaries to Hawaii's queen.","start":310.77,"end":317.11,"words":[[310.82,311.5],[311.5,311.88],[311.88,312.41],[312.41,312.64],[312.9,313.21],[313.21,313.74],[313.74,313.96],[313.96,314.12],[314.12,314.57],[314.57,315.56],[315.56,315.71],[315.71,316.24],[316.24,316.62]]},{"el":"s6","text":"The rest of the Learn modules follow these tools into American institutions.","start":317.46,"end":322.46,"words":[[317.5,317.65],[317.65,317.96],[317.96,318.11],[318.11,318.27],[318.27,318.5],[318.5,319.03],[319.03,319.42],[319.42,319.65],[319.65,319.95],[319.95,320.26],[320.26,320.88],[320.88,321.87]]}]}